\n \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/3833525afb515fbf56c616b5ae5470247fc38fa5?fields=paperId%2Ctitle%2Cyear%2Cvenue%2CcitationCount%2CcitationStyles%2Curl\",\n \"title\": \"On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Accommodate Ties in Paired Comparison Experiments\",\n \"venue\": \"\",\n \"year\": 1970\n}\nbuild_claims.py\nbuild_figures.py\n200","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HDeAkJgDmMSnnEZU18EZ7c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass{article}\n2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n16\t}\n17\t% --- end watermark ---\n18\t % For LaTeX2e\n19\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n20\t\n21\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n22\t\\usepackage{url}\n23\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n24\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n25\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n26\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n27\t\n28\t\\newcommand{\\ts}{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\n29\t\\newcommand{\\sig}{\\sigma}\n30\t\n31\t\\title{How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Probe on Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data}\n32\t\\author{Anonymous authors}\n33\t\n34\t\\begin{document}\n35\t\\maketitle\n36\t\n37\t\\begin{abstract}\n38\tPairwise-comparison leaderboards rank competitors by fitting a Bradley--Terry model to many ``A versus B'' judgments, yet it is unclear how few comparisons must change before the top-ranked competitor loses its place, and whether this differs between evaluation designs.\n39\tWe measure this for a ridge-penalised Bradley--Terry fit, a single target (the rank-1 competitor), two edit modes (dropping rows or reversing outcomes) and budgets from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of rows.\n40\tEdits are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search and scored by a trusted evaluator that refits the model on the edited data.\n41\tOn held-out data from four designs (crowd votes, expert human judgments, LLM-judge judgments, basketball results) the family-averaged edit success score is 0.606, with per-design scores of 1.000, 0.833, 0.374 and 0.216, respectively.\n42\tAt the 2\\% budget the search displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, in no LLM-judge case, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes.\n43\tA simple win-rate heuristic scores 0.574.\n44\tThese are bounded measurements of one heuristic attack on one target; they carry no sampling uncertainty, and the cause of the design ordering is untested.\n45\t\\end{abstract}\n46\t\n47\t\\section{Introduction}\n48\tPublic model-evaluation leaderboards often aggregate a large number of pairwise judgments (which of two responses, models or teams was better) into a Bradley--Terry (BT) score for every competitor, and then publish a ranking \\citep{Bradley1952Rank,Chiang2024Chatbot}.\n49\tThese rankings guide model selection, release announcements and research comparisons, so their dependence on the underlying comparison data matters.\n50\tTwo lines of concern motivate this paper.\n51\tFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\n52\tSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\n53\tBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n54\t\n55\tWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\n56\tThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\n57\tA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\n58\tThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n59\tWe apply it to held-out data from four evaluation designs that differ in who judges and what is judged: crowd-sourced human votes on chatbot responses \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot}, expert human judgments and GPT-4 judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}, and National Basketball Association (NBA) game results.\n60\tFigure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n61\t\n62\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n63\t\\centering\n64\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.png}\n65\t\\caption{Evaluation protocol. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fitted with a ridge Bradley--Terry model, which fixes the rank-1 competitor and its gap to the runner-up. A proposer returns at most $k$ rows to drop or reverse, with $k$ from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows. The evaluator refits the edited data, reports whether rank 1 changed, and otherwise reports the fraction of the gap closed.}\n66\t\\label{fig:overview}\n67\t\\end{figure}\n68\t\n69\t\\paragraph{What this paper is and is not.}\n70\tThe studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n71\tThree alternative search variants were designed but produced no scored evidence and therefore support no comparison (Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}).\n72\tThe contribution is a controlled, verified measurement of how a fixed adversarial probe fares on four evaluation designs, together with a careful statement of what that measurement does and does not show.\n73\tConcretely:\n74\t\\begin{itemize}\n75\t\\item We specify an operational, evaluator-verified protocol (exact refit, five budgets, two edit modes, one target) that makes rank-1 vulnerability comparable across designs, and we derive the first-order effect of dropping or reversing a row, including an identity relating the two modes (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n76\t\\item We report that the probe's success is strongly design dependent: edit success scores of 1.000 (crowd), 0.833 (sports), 0.374 (expert human) and 0.216 (LLM judge), and 2\\%-budget displacement in all crowd and sports cases but in no LLM-judge case (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n77\t\\item We document the validity checks that were run and the uncertainty that is unavailable, and we separate what the numbers show from hypotheses about the cause of the ordering, none of which was tested (Sections~\\ref{sec:analysis} and~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n78\t\\end{itemize}\n79\t\n80\t\\section{Related Work}\n81\t\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.}\n82\tThe BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, multivariate responses \\citep{Davidson1969MultivariatePC}, two-dimensional score structure \\citep{Causeur2005A2E}, sparse grouped comparisons \\citep{Yan2011GROUPEDSP}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\n83\t\n84\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty of rankings.}\n85\tStatistical work quantifies how sampling noise affects BT scores and ranks, through confidence regions for the score vector \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, hypothesis tests \\citep{Makur2024MinimaxHT} and estimators of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons \\citep{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP}. These analyses describe average-case sampling variation under a generative model. We instead ask a worst-case, data-conditional question about a single observed dataset: whether a small number of specific rows can change the top rank. The two views are complementary, and our measurements do not substitute for either confidence intervals or noise calibration.\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Influence and robustness to dropping data.}\n88\tInfluence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n89\t\n90\t\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n91\t\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n92\t\n93\t\\section{Problem Setting and Method}\n94\t\\label{sec:method}\n95\t\\subsection{Leaderboard, target and edit modes}\n96\tA comparison dataset is a list of $n$ rows $(a_i,b_i,y_i)$ over $K$ competitors, with $y_i\\in\\{+1,-1,0\\}$ meaning that $a_i$ wins, $b_i$ wins, or the pair tied. Write $\\tilde y_i\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$ for the corresponding win score of $a_i$. The evaluator fits $\\ts\\in\\mathbb{R}^K$ by maximising the penalised log-likelihood\n97\t\\begin{equation}\n98\t\\ell(\\ts)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Big[\\tilde y_i\\log\\sig(\\theta_{a_i}-\\theta_{b_i})+(1-\\tilde y_i)\\log\\sig(\\theta_{b_i}-\\theta_{a_i})\\Big]-\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\sum_{k}\\theta_k^2,\\qquad \\lambda=10^{-3},\n99\t\\label{eq:bt}\n100\t\\end{equation}\n101\tto gradient tolerance $10^{-10}$. A tie therefore counts as half a win for each side. The rank-1 competitor is $\\mathrm{top}=\\arg\\max_k\\hat\\theta_k$, the runner-up is $\\mathrm{rv}=\\arg\\max_{k\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\hat\\theta_k$, and the \\emph{gap} is $G(\\ts)=\\theta_{\\mathrm{top}}-\\max_{j\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\theta_j$. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\n102\tThe target is to make the unedited rank-1 competitor no longer rank first after the edit, that is, to drive $G$ below zero on the refit of the edited data.\n103\tThere are two edit modes. In \\emph{drop} mode, the chosen rows are removed. In \\emph{flip} mode, the outcomes of the chosen rows are reversed, which is only allowed for decisive rows ($y_i\\neq 0$). We call the second mode ``reversal'' in prose and use the evaluator's name ``flip'' only for the mode label, to avoid confusion with displacement rates.\n104\tThe budget is $k=\\max(1,\\lceil fn\\rceil)$ with $f\\in\\{0.1\\%,0.25\\%,0.5\\%,1\\%,2\\%\\}$ of the rows.\\footnote{The five budget fractions are fixed in the evaluator's budget ladder.} % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#LADDER]]\n105\tThe proposer receives the raw arrays with anonymised competitor indices and returns at most $k$ distinct row indices; any malformed answer scores zero for that budget.\n106\t\n107\t\\subsection{First-order influence edit search}\n108\t\\label{sec:influence}\n109\tThe studied search fits $\\hat\\ts$ once, and then ranks rows by the first-order change of the gap that editing each row would cause. Let $p_i=\\sig(\\hat\\theta_{a_i}-\\hat\\theta_{b_i})$ and let $A=\\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})^{\\top}+\\lambda I$ be the negative Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt}. The score gradient contributed by row $i$ is $(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$. Removing the row perturbs the stationarity condition by $-(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$, so a Newton step gives the parameter change $A^{-1}$ times that perturbation. Define the sensitivity direction and the row coefficient\n110\t\\begin{equation}\n111\tv=A^{-1}(e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}),\\qquad s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}.\n112\t\\end{equation}\n113\tThe first-order change of the (top versus runner-up) gap is then\n114\t\\begin{equation}\n115\t\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{drop}}=-s_i\\,(\\tilde y_i-p_i),\\qquad\n116\t\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{flip}}=s_i\\,(1-2\\tilde y_i),\n117\t\\label{eq:effects}\n118\t\\end{equation}\n119\twhere the second expression is the effect of replacing $\\tilde y_i$ by $1-\\tilde y_i$. The search returns the (at most) $k$ rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ among admissible rows (all rows for drop, decisive rows for flip), discarding rows with $\\Delta_i\\ge 0$ and breaking ties by row index, so the output is deterministic. The implementation fits $\\hat\\ts$ by Newton iterations on the $K\\times K$ pair-count matrix, then solves a single $K\\times K$ linear system for $v$. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\n120\t\n121\tThree properties of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} shape how the results should be read.\n122\t\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row.\n123\t\\emph{(ii) A fixed rival.} The direction is built for the initial runner-up only. If an edit set raises a different competitor above the incumbent, the search does not anticipate it, although the evaluator would credit the displacement.\n124\t\\emph{(iii) Flip versus drop.} Reversing a row is equivalent to dropping it and inserting the opposite outcome, so $\\Delta^{\\mathrm{flip}}_i=\\Delta^{\\mathrm{drop}}_i+s_i\\,((1-\\tilde y_i)-p_i)$. For a decisive row, $\\tilde y_i-p_i$ has the sign of $\\tilde y_i-\\tfrac12$, so the two modes select gap-reducing rows by the same sign condition on $s_i$, and the flip effect is larger by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|>1$. At first order, reversal is therefore the stronger edit for the same number of rows; this is a statement about Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects}, not an empirical finding.\n125\t\n126\t\\subsection{Scoring and verification}\n127\tFor each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Reference policies and controls}\n130\tThree evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#baseline_scores]]\n131\tA \\emph{permuted-input control} re-runs the proposer on a row-shuffled, competitor-relabelled copy of each case, maps the proposed rows back to the original data and scores them with the same refit; the reported statistic is the mean absolute difference from the unpermuted score. The \\emph{valid proposal rate} is the fraction of budget-level answers that passed format checks.\n132\t\n133\t\\subsection{Evaluation designs}\n134\t\\label{sec:designs}\n135\tTable~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (verified by content hashes in the task protocol), and anonymised competitor indices carry no model or team names. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#provenance]]\n136\t\n137\t\\begin{table}[t]\n138\t\\centering\n139\t\\caption{Evaluation designs of the sealed final evaluation. Cases are (data source $\\times$ edit mode). Dataset sizes are described qualitatively only; no row counts are used in this paper.}\n140\t\\label{tab:designs}\n141\t\\small\n142\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.0cm}p{5.5cm}p{1.5cm}p{2.7cm}}\n143\t\\toprule\n144\tDesign & Data source & Cases & Competitors \\\\\n145\t\\midrule\n146\tCrowd votes & Held-out partition of public Chatbot Arena human-preference votes \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot} & 2 (drop, flip) & Many chatbots; the largest dataset \\\\\n147\tExpert human & MT-Bench expert human pairwise judgments \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}; ties kept & 2 & Six models \\\\\n148\tLLM judge & GPT-4 pairwise judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons; two tie labels mapped to a tie & 2 & Six models \\\\\n149\tSports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n150\t\\bottomrule\n151\t\\end{tabular}\n152\t\\end{table}\n153\t\n154\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol and reporting}\n155\tThe proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never compared with the final scores.\n156\t\n157\t\\section{Results}\n158\t\\label{sec:results}\n159\tAll numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation, whose qualification outcome is \\emph{qualified} with status \\emph{completed}. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\n160\tQualification certifies that the run was complete and valid (full declared transcript, trusted-refit scoring, non-vacuous controls, all reference policies computed); it is not evidence of superiority or of generality. Candidate-reported diagnostics are marked unverified by the evaluator and are not used here. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n161\t\n162\t\\subsection{Edit success differs strongly across evaluation designs}\n163\tThe family-averaged edit success score of the first-order influence search is $0.606$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\n164\tPanel (a) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} shows the per-design scores: crowd votes $1.000$, sports $0.833$, expert human $0.374$ and LLM judge $0.216$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_crowd]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_sports]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_expert_human]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_llm_judge]]\n165\tBecause a level scores at most one and exactly one only when rank 1 is displaced (up to an exact tie), a design score of $1.000$ means that rank 1 was displaced at every one of the five budgets, including the smallest (0.1\\% of rows), in both crowd cases. The sports score of $0.833<1$ implies that at least one smaller budget in at least one sports case did not displace rank 1. For the LLM judge, rank 1 was not displaced at the 2\\% budget in either mode (Table~\\ref{tab:disp}), so the score of $0.216$ reflects partial closure of the gap.\n166\t\n167\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n168\t\\centering\n169\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/design_scores.pdf}\n170\t\\caption{Edit success score on the sealed final data (higher means rank 1 was displaced more reliably or a larger fraction of the gap was closed). (a) The first-order influence search for each evaluation design. (b) Family-averaged scores (equal weights over the four designs) for the evaluator's reference policies and the studied search; blue marks the studied search. Each bar is a single deterministic evaluation value; no uncertainty interval exists.}\n171\t\\label{fig:scores}\n172\t\\end{figure}\n173\t\n174\t\\subsection{Top-1 displacement at the largest budget}\n175\tTable~\\ref{tab:disp} reports the displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by design and edit mode. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_crowd_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_expert_human_flip]]\n176\tRank 1 is displaced in every crowd and sports case in both modes. For expert human data only reversal succeeds, and for the LLM judge neither mode does. Because each crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge cell contains a single case and the proposer is deterministic, every entry is $0$ or $1$; the sports entries average three seasons and also happen to be $0$ or $1$.\n177\tThe ordering by edit success score (crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge) is thus mirrored by the number of design--mode cells in which the heuristic reaches displacement at 2\\% (2, 2, 1, 0 of 2).\n178\t\n179\t\\begin{table}[t]\n180\t\\centering\n181\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: per-design edit success score and top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by edit mode. Entries in the displacement columns are the means over cases and seeds in the cell (one case for crowd, expert human and LLM judge; three seasons for sports).}\n182\t\\label{tab:disp}\n183\t\\small\n184\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n185\t\\toprule\n186\tDesign & Cases & Edit success & Displaced at 2\\%, drop & Displaced at 2\\%, flip \\\\\n187\t\\midrule\n188\tCrowd votes  & 2 & 1.000 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_crowd_flip]]\n189\tSports       & 6 & 0.833 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_flip]]\n190\tExpert human & 2 & 0.374 & 0.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_expert_human_drop]]\n191\tLLM judge    & 2 & 0.216 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip]]\n192\t\\midrule\n193\tFamily average & 12 & 0.606 & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{not defined} \\\\\n194\t\\bottomrule\n195\t\\end{tabular}\n196\t\\end{table}\n197\t\n198\t\\subsection{Reference policies set the scale}\n199\tPanel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small fraction of the gap. This suggests that the displacement obtained in crowd and sports data owes more to targeted row selection than to perturbation in general, although per-design values for the random policy are not available to check it. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_constant]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_random]]\n200\tThe win-rate heuristic, which needs no model refit, scores $0.574$ against $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_winrate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_influence]]\n201\tThis is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search (both score $0.606$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.margin_vs_best_baseline]]\n202\tThe task protocol declares a separate claim of outperforming the reference policies, which requires a positive margin and a paired-bootstrap interval excluding zero; that claim is not met here. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#protocol.promotion_rule]]\n203\t\n204\t\\subsection{Validity checks and run-to-run behaviour}\n205\tThe valid proposal rate is $1.0$: every answer to every budget level passed the format checks. The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$, so on the row-shuffled, relabelled copies the proposer obtained the same scores as on the original order, and the measured scores do not depend on row order or competitor labels. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.valid_proposal_rate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.perm_control_abs_gap]]\n206\tThe three evaluation seeds produce identical family-averaged scores ($0.6057$ each), as expected for a deterministic proposer on fixed data. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n207\tThis agreement should not be read as low variability of the leaderboard under resampling: no bootstrap, subsampling or noise model was applied to the data, so we report no confidence interval.\n208\t\n209\t\\section{What the Evidence Does and Does Not Show}\n210\t\\label{sec:analysis}\n211\t\\subsection{A measured ordering, not a general sensitivity law}\n212\tThe measurement supports the statement that, with this heuristic, within these budgets and on these twelve cases, rank-1 vulnerability decreases in the order crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge. The ordering is an observation for one attack and one target. Failure to displace rank 1 (LLM judge in both modes, expert-human data in drop mode) shows only that this one-shot first-order heuristic did not succeed within 2\\% of the rows. It is not a certificate that no successful edit set exists, and a stronger search could close the gap in cells where this one did not. Conversely, success in crowd and sports data shows that rank 1 can be displaced with at most the budget used, not that it is the smallest such edit set.\n213\t\n214\t\\subsection{Candidate explanations, all untested}\n215\tThe artifacts contain no experiment that connects the ordering to a mechanism. We list candidate explanations to guide follow-up work and label each as a hypothesis.\n216\t\\begin{itemize}\n217\t\\item \\emph{Margin relative to data mass.} The relative size of the rank-1 gap compared with the total information in the data determines how many rows must move; designs with a thin top margin would be easier to dislodge. Gaps and row counts per design are not available to us, so this is unchecked.\n218\t\\item \\emph{Concentration of influence.} If the coefficients $s_i$ concentrate on few pairs (for example, matches between the top two competitors), few edits can matter; how concentrated they are depends on the comparison graph of each design, which we did not inspect.\n219\t\\item \\emph{Number of competitors and graph density.} MT-Bench data involve six models, whereas the crowd and sports data involve more competitors. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\n220\t\\item \\emph{Tie handling.} Ties count as half a win and cannot be reversed in flip mode. Designs with many ties have fewer rows available for reversal; we did not tabulate tie fractions, so we cannot say whether this matters here.\n221\t\\item \\emph{Linearisation error.} A 2\\% edit of a small dataset is a larger perturbation of $A$ and $p$ than a 2\\% edit of a large one, so the additive first-order approximation may degrade differently across designs.\n222\t\\end{itemize}\n223\tDesigns are confounded: they differ at the same time in dataset size, number of competitors, rank-1 margin, tie rate and judge type, and each design has few cases. No single factor can be isolated from this experiment, and the sequentially tuned, single-configuration setup provides no ablations.\n224\t\n225\t\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\n226\tExpert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (including the second tie label in the GPT-4 data), and the size of the rank-1 gap are all plausible, and each design has one case per mode, so this is a difference between two single measurements and not a statistical result about human versus LLM judges.\n227\t\n228\t\\subsection{Drop versus reversal}\n229\tIn the one design where the two modes differ (expert human), reversal succeeds and dropping does not. This is consistent with property (iii) of Section~\\ref{sec:influence}: for the same rows, reversal has a larger first-order effect than removal by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|$. It remains a single-cell observation: in crowd and sports data both modes displace rank 1 and in LLM-judge data neither does, so the mode matters in only one of the four designs.\n230\t\n231\t\\subsection{What a stronger test of sensitivity would need}\n232\tOur results leave the original question (sensitivity to small changes from noise or from intent) only partly answered. Answering it would require, at least: edit-size curves for minimal displacing sets from a search with a verified lower-bound or certificate, not only a one-shot heuristic; noise calibration against resampled data (for example, comparing edit sizes with the sampling variability of the gap); several cases per design with confidence intervals; and ablations over subsample size and tie handling. None of these was performed here, and we state them as open rather than as completed work.\n233\t\n234\t\\section{Limitations}\n235\t\\label{sec:limits}\n236\t\\paragraph{One attack, one target.} Only a one-shot first-order search with a single rival direction at the initial fit is evaluated, and only the displacement of rank 1 is targeted. Other targets (top-$m$ sets, pairwise order of two chosen competitors) and iterative or batched searches are not covered.\n237\t\\paragraph{Fixed leaderboard specification.} The evaluator's BT specification (ridge $10^{-3}$, tie as half a win) is part of the measurement. We do not know how conclusions change for other regularisation strengths, tie models or rating systems.\n238\t\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.\n239\t\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty and no noise-versus-intent attribution.} The identical seeds reflect determinism, and no resampling was performed; we therefore cannot say whether the observed vulnerability exceeds ordinary sampling variation of the leaderboard, nor whether it corresponds to plausible noise or to deliberate manipulation. We also do not model what an adversary could actually do on a real platform.\n240\t\\paragraph{Missing diagnostics and aggregate-only baselines.} Per-case gaps, row counts, budget-level scores and per-design values for the reference policies were not available to the analysis, and the evaluator marks candidate diagnostics as unverified. The comparison with the win-rate heuristic is therefore a single aggregate difference.\n241\t\\paragraph{No improved search.} Three alternative search designs were not scored (Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}), so no statement about improved or minimal edit sets can be drawn.\n242\t\n243\t\\section{Conclusion}\n244\tWe measured, with exact-refit verification, how a one-shot first-order influence search fares at displacing the rank-1 competitor of a ridge Bradley--Terry leaderboard using 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows, on crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and sports data. The family-averaged edit success is 0.606, and it varies strongly by design: 1.000 for crowd votes, 0.833 for sports, 0.374 for expert human data and 0.216 for the LLM judge. At 2\\% the heuristic displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, never for the LLM judge, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes. A win-rate heuristic reaches 0.574, a difference too small to rank the two policies without uncertainty estimates. Qualification of the evaluation attests to its validity, not to superiority or generality; the margin over the best reference policy is zero because the studied search is the evaluator's influence baseline.\n245\tThe result is a bounded measurement for one probe, one target and four datasets. It shows that rank-1 vulnerability under this attack is highly heterogeneous across evaluation designs, which argues against reporting a single sensitivity number for leaderboards. It does not show why, does not separate noise from intent, and does not show minimal edit sizes. Resampling-based calibration, several cases per design and stronger searches are the natural next steps.\n246\t\n247\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n248\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n249\t\\clearpage\n250\t\\bibliography{references}\n251\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n252\t\n253\t\\clearpage\n254\t\\appendix\n255\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n256\t\n257\t\\subsection{Evaluator Specification and Metric Definitions}\n258\t\\label{app:eval}\n259\tThe evaluation interface is a request--response protocol with a reset operation (clearing all proposer state and seeding it) and a propose operation. Each propose request carries the mode, the budget $k$, the number of competitors, the number of rows and base64-encoded integer arrays of competitor indices and outcomes, with rows in the order of the original data. Each call has a hard wall-clock limit of 30 seconds, and the proposer runs on CPU with two threads. Responses with extra keys, non-integer or duplicate or out-of-range indices, more than $k$ indices, or tie rows in flip mode are invalid and score zero for that level. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\n260\tThe sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#CASE_SPECS]]\n261\tDesign scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case, and the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\n262\t\n263\t\\subsection{Complete Final Metric Table}\n264\t\\label{app:metrics}\n265\tTable~\\ref{tab:allmetrics} lists all retained sealed-final metrics.\n266\t\\begin{table}[h]\n267\t\\centering\n268\t\\caption{All retained metrics of the sealed final evaluation (three evaluation seeds, identical across seeds). Displacement rates are at the 2\\% budget.}\n269\t\\label{tab:allmetrics}\n270\t\\small\n271\t\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n272\t\\toprule\n273\tMetric & Value \\\\\n274\t\\midrule\n275\tFamily-averaged edit success (influence search) & 0.6057 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\n276\tEdit success: crowd / sports & 1.0000 / 0.8328 \\\\\n277\tEdit success: expert human / LLM judge & 0.3742 / 0.2157 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n278\tDisplacement, drop: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 0 / 0 \\\\\n279\tDisplacement, flip: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 1 / 0 \\\\\n280\tReference policy, constant (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\n281\tReference policy, random (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\n282\tReference policy, win-rate heuristic (family-averaged) & 0.5739 \\\\\n283\tReference policy, influence baseline (family-averaged) & 0.6057 \\\\\n284\tMargin versus best reference policy & 0.0000 \\\\\n285\tPermuted-input control, mean absolute gap & 0.0000 \\\\\n286\tValid proposal rate & 1.0000 \\\\\n287\t\\bottomrule\n288\t\\end{tabular}\n289\t\\end{table}\n290\t\n291\t\\subsection{Seeded Evaluation Records}\n292\t\\label{app:seeds}\n293\tThree seed-level records were produced (seeds 201, 202, 203), each with the identical family-averaged score $0.6057$ and recorded durations of $23.1$, $11.8$ and $11.6$ seconds. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n294\tThe identical scores are expected: the proposer is deterministic, a seed never selects data or workload, and the anonymisation of competitor indices is fixed for each case by the evaluator. Durations vary by a factor of two between records and we do not attribute the difference to any cause. The evaluator labels candidate-produced diagnostics as unverified; none of our statements relies on them, and all quantities are computed by the trusted refit.\n295\t\n296\t\\subsection{Algorithm: First-Order Influence Edit Search}\n297\t\\label{app:algo}\n298\tGiven the arrays, mode and budget $k$:\n299\t\\begin{enumerate}\n300\t\\item Aggregate rows into a $K\\times K$ weighted win-count matrix (a tie adds $\\tfrac12$ to each direction) and solve Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} by Newton iterations (at most 100) until the largest gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$.\n301\t\\item Take $\\mathrm{top}$ and $\\mathrm{rv}$ as the two largest scores, and solve $A v=e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}$.\n302\t\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n303\t\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n304\t\\end{enumerate}\n305\tThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline share this logic. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\n306\tIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n307\t\n308\t\\subsection{Selection Context and Unscored Search Variants}\n309\t\\label{app:selection}\n310\tThe configuration studied here was chosen on a separate search data collection, on which it obtained a search-scope score of $0.914$ with a score delta of $0.0$ relative to the baseline (which is the selected configuration); the study outcome is recorded as completed without a qualifying winner and the selection kind is the baseline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\n311\tThree search variants were proposed as potential improvements: a calibrated minimal-perturbation sensitivity analysis across designs, a pair-class aggregated multi-rival greedy search with exact-refit selection, and rival-specific chunked re-linearised edit plans with an exact-refit prefix line search. All three produced no score ($3$ total, $0$ scored, $3$ unscored). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\n312\tUnscored variants are missing performance evidence, not measured losses, and no comparison involving them appears anywhere in this paper. The search-scope score $0.914$ and the final score $0.606$ come from different data collections and scopes, with different designs (the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must not be read as a decline or a generalisation gap.\n313\t\n314\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n315\t\\label{app:llm}\n316\tLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored; the studied search is the task's starter code. An LLM agent retrieved references through a literature-search API, planned the paper, wrote this manuscript and its claim-to-evidence mapping from the recorded evaluation artifacts, and produced the figures. The evaluation protocol, data, evaluator and scoring were fixed by the task specification before the run, and every number in the paper is read from the evaluator's recorded outputs. The starter references named in the task were taken from the task description.\n317\t\n318\t\\end{document}\n319\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de3ecb5e-ff4d-4189-a40b-6bc7f84aa456","uuid":"8cc9f372-4ce4-4585-b927-7d4c0280ece9","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:44:11.104Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\ts}{\\boldsymbol{\\theta}}\n\\newcommand{\\sig}{\\sigma}\n\n\\title{How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Probe on Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nPairwise-comparison leaderboards rank competitors by fitting a Bradley--Terry model to many ``A versus B'' judgments, yet it is unclear how few comparisons must change before the top-ranked competitor loses its place, and whether this differs between evaluation designs.\nWe measure this for a ridge-penalised Bradley--Terry fit, a single target (the rank-1 competitor), two edit modes (dropping rows or reversing outcomes) and budgets from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of rows.\nEdits are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search and scored by a trusted evaluator that refits the model on the edited data.\nOn held-out data from four designs (crowd votes, expert human judgments, LLM-judge judgments, basketball results) the family-averaged edit success score is 0.606, with per-design scores of 1.000, 0.833, 0.374 and 0.216, respectively.\nAt the 2\\% budget the search displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, in no LLM-judge case, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes.\nA simple win-rate heuristic scores 0.574.\nThese are bounded measurements of one heuristic attack on one target; they carry no sampling uncertainty, and the cause of the design ordering is untested.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nPublic model-evaluation leaderboards often aggregate a large number of pairwise judgments (which of two responses, models or teams was better) into a Bradley--Terry (BT) score for every competitor, and then publish a ranking \\citep{Bradley1952Rank,Chiang2024Chatbot}.\nThese rankings guide model selection, release announcements and research comparisons, so their dependence on the underlying comparison data matters.\nTwo lines of concern motivate this paper.\nFirst, data are noisy and finite, so a small difference between the top two competitors may not be reliable \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}.\nSecond, crowd-sourced leaderboards can be influenced deliberately, for instance through vote rigging \\citep{Min2025Rigging} or through differences in data access and sampling between providers \\citep{Singh2025Illusion}.\nBoth concerns lead to the same operational question: \\emph{how many comparison rows have to be edited before the top-ranked competitor is no longer ranked first?}\n\nWe study this question for a deliberately narrow, fully specified setting.\nThe leaderboard is a ridge-penalised BT fit; the target is the competitor ranked first on the unedited data; an edit either drops a row or reverses its outcome; and the edit budget is between 0.1\\% and 2\\% of the rows.\nA trusted evaluator, which never uses any quantity reported by the proposer, refits the model on the edited data and records whether rank 1 was displaced or, if not, what fraction of the top-versus-runner-up gap was closed.\nThe edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\nWe apply it to held-out data from four evaluation designs that differ in who judges and what is judged: crowd-sourced human votes on chatbot responses \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot}, expert human judgments and GPT-4 judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}, and National Basketball Association (NBA) game results.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:overview} summarises the protocol.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_protocol.png}\n\\caption{Evaluation protocol. Comparison rows from four evaluation designs are fitted with a ridge Bradley--Terry model, which fixes the rank-1 competitor and its gap to the runner-up. A proposer returns at most $k$ rows to drop or reverse, with $k$ from 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows. The evaluator refits the edited data, reports whether rank 1 changed, and otherwise reports the fraction of the gap closed.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\paragraph{What this paper is and is not.}\nThe studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\nThree alternative search variants were designed but produced no scored evidence and therefore support no comparison (Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}).\nThe contribution is a controlled, verified measurement of how a fixed adversarial probe fares on four evaluation designs, together with a careful statement of what that measurement does and does not show.\nConcretely:\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item We specify an operational, evaluator-verified protocol (exact refit, five budgets, two edit modes, one target) that makes rank-1 vulnerability comparable across designs, and we derive the first-order effect of dropping or reversing a row, including an identity relating the two modes (Section~\\ref{sec:method}).\n\\item We report that the probe's success is strongly design dependent: edit success scores of 1.000 (crowd), 0.833 (sports), 0.374 (expert human) and 0.216 (LLM judge), and 2\\%-budget displacement in all crowd and sports cases but in no LLM-judge case (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\item We document the validity checks that were run and the uncertainty that is unavailable, and we separate what the numbers show from hypotheses about the cause of the ordering, none of which was tested (Sections~\\ref{sec:analysis} and~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Bradley--Terry models and their estimation.}\nThe BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, multivariate responses \\citep{Davidson1969MultivariatePC}, two-dimensional score structure \\citep{Causeur2005A2E}, sparse grouped comparisons \\citep{Yan2011GROUPEDSP}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temporal drift \\citep{Tian2023ASA}, continuous outcomes \\citep{Stern2011ModeratedPC,Fageot2023GeneralizedBM} and Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. The model has also been used to derive priorities from comparison matrices \\citep{Genest1999DerivingPF} and has been related to PageRank \\citep{Selby2024PageRankAT}. Maximum likelihood estimation requires conditions on the comparison graph \\citep{Butler2004TheEO,Bong2021GeneralizedRF}, which motivates penalised fits \\citep{Varin2024TractableRR}; our evaluator uses a ridge penalty and counts a tie as half a win for each side, a convention in the spirit of tie-extended models. Algorithms for fitting generalised BT models have been analysed by \\citet{Vojnovic2019ConvergenceRO}, and the large-sample behaviour of the estimator when the number of competitors grows by \\citet{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT} and \\citet{Li2021ThePO}.\n\n\\paragraph{Uncertainty of rankings.}\nStatistical work quantifies how sampling noise affects BT scores and ranks, through confidence regions for the score vector \\citep{Gao2021UncertaintyQI}, hypothesis tests \\citep{Makur2024MinimaxHT} and estimators of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons \\citep{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP}. These analyses describe average-case sampling variation under a generative model. We instead ask a worst-case, data-conditional question about a single observed dataset: whether a small number of specific rows can change the top rank. The two views are complementary, and our measurements do not substitute for either confidence intervals or noise calibration.\n\n\\paragraph{Influence and robustness to dropping data.}\nInfluence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n\n\\section{Problem Setting and Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\\subsection{Leaderboard, target and edit modes}\nA comparison dataset is a list of $n$ rows $(a_i,b_i,y_i)$ over $K$ competitors, with $y_i\\in\\{+1,-1,0\\}$ meaning that $a_i$ wins, $b_i$ wins, or the pair tied. Write $\\tilde y_i\\in\\{1,\\tfrac12,0\\}$ for the corresponding win score of $a_i$. The evaluator fits $\\ts\\in\\mathbb{R}^K$ by maximising the penalised log-likelihood\n\\begin{equation}\n\\ell(\\ts)=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Big[\\tilde y_i\\log\\sig(\\theta_{a_i}-\\theta_{b_i})+(1-\\tilde y_i)\\log\\sig(\\theta_{b_i}-\\theta_{a_i})\\Big]-\\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\sum_{k}\\theta_k^2,\\qquad \\lambda=10^{-3},\n\\label{eq:bt}\n\\end{equation}\nto gradient tolerance $10^{-10}$. A tie therefore counts as half a win for each side. The rank-1 competitor is $\\mathrm{top}=\\arg\\max_k\\hat\\theta_k$, the runner-up is $\\mathrm{rv}=\\arg\\max_{k\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\hat\\theta_k$, and the \\emph{gap} is $G(\\ts)=\\theta_{\\mathrm{top}}-\\max_{j\\neq \\mathrm{top}}\\theta_j$. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\nThe target is to make the unedited rank-1 competitor no longer rank first after the edit, that is, to drive $G$ below zero on the refit of the edited data.\nThere are two edit modes. In \\emph{drop} mode, the chosen rows are removed. In \\emph{flip} mode, the outcomes of the chosen rows are reversed, which is only allowed for decisive rows ($y_i\\neq 0$). We call the second mode ``reversal'' in prose and use the evaluator's name ``flip'' only for the mode label, to avoid confusion with displacement rates.\nThe budget is $k=\\max(1,\\lceil fn\\rceil)$ with $f\\in\\{0.1\\%,0.25\\%,0.5\\%,1\\%,2\\%\\}$ of the rows.\\footnote{The five budget fractions are fixed in the evaluator's budget ladder.} % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#LADDER]]\nThe proposer receives the raw arrays with anonymised competitor indices and returns at most $k$ distinct row indices; any malformed answer scores zero for that budget.\n\n\\subsection{First-order influence edit search}\n\\label{sec:influence}\nThe studied search fits $\\hat\\ts$ once, and then ranks rows by the first-order change of the gap that editing each row would cause. Let $p_i=\\sig(\\hat\\theta_{a_i}-\\hat\\theta_{b_i})$ and let $A=\\sum_i p_i(1-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})^{\\top}+\\lambda I$ be the negative Hessian of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt}. The score gradient contributed by row $i$ is $(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$. Removing the row perturbs the stationarity condition by $-(\\tilde y_i-p_i)(e_{a_i}-e_{b_i})$, so a Newton step gives the parameter change $A^{-1}$ times that perturbation. Define the sensitivity direction and the row coefficient\n\\begin{equation}\nv=A^{-1}(e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}),\\qquad s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe first-order change of the (top versus runner-up) gap is then\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{drop}}=-s_i\\,(\\tilde y_i-p_i),\\qquad\n\\Delta_i^{\\mathrm{flip}}=s_i\\,(1-2\\tilde y_i),\n\\label{eq:effects}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere the second expression is the effect of replacing $\\tilde y_i$ by $1-\\tilde y_i$. The search returns the (at most) $k$ rows with the most negative $\\Delta_i$ among admissible rows (all rows for drop, decisive rows for flip), discarding rows with $\\Delta_i\\ge 0$ and breaking ties by row index, so the output is deterministic. The implementation fits $\\hat\\ts$ by Newton iterations on the $K\\times K$ pair-count matrix, then solves a single $K\\times K$ linear system for $v$. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\n\nThree properties of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} shape how the results should be read.\n\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row.\n\\emph{(ii) A fixed rival.} The direction is built for the initial runner-up only. If an edit set raises a different competitor above the incumbent, the search does not anticipate it, although the evaluator would credit the displacement.\n\\emph{(iii) Flip versus drop.} Reversing a row is equivalent to dropping it and inserting the opposite outcome, so $\\Delta^{\\mathrm{flip}}_i=\\Delta^{\\mathrm{drop}}_i+s_i\\,((1-\\tilde y_i)-p_i)$. For a decisive row, $\\tilde y_i-p_i$ has the sign of $\\tilde y_i-\\tfrac12$, so the two modes select gap-reducing rows by the same sign condition on $s_i$, and the flip effect is larger by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|>1$. At first order, reversal is therefore the stronger edit for the same number of rows; this is a statement about Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects}, not an empirical finding.\n\n\\subsection{Scoring and verification}\nFor each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\n\n\\subsection{Reference policies and controls}\nThree evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#baseline_scores]]\nA \\emph{permuted-input control} re-runs the proposer on a row-shuffled, competitor-relabelled copy of each case, maps the proposed rows back to the original data and scores them with the same refit; the reported statistic is the mean absolute difference from the unpermuted score. The \\emph{valid proposal rate} is the fraction of budget-level answers that passed format checks.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation designs}\n\\label{sec:designs}\nTable~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (verified by content hashes in the task protocol), and anonymised competitor indices carry no model or team names. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#provenance]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Evaluation designs of the sealed final evaluation. Cases are (data source $\\times$ edit mode). Dataset sizes are described qualitatively only; no row counts are used in this paper.}\n\\label{tab:designs}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{p{2.0cm}p{5.5cm}p{1.5cm}p{2.7cm}}\n\\toprule\nDesign & Data source & Cases & Competitors \\\\\n\\midrule\nCrowd votes & Held-out partition of public Chatbot Arena human-preference votes \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot} & 2 (drop, flip) & Many chatbots; the largest dataset \\\\\nExpert human & MT-Bench expert human pairwise judgments \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}; ties kept & 2 & Six models \\\\\nLLM judge & GPT-4 pairwise judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons; two tie labels mapped to a tie & 2 & Six models \\\\\nSports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol and reporting}\nThe proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never compared with the final scores.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\nAll numbers in this section come from the sealed final evaluation, whose qualification outcome is \\emph{qualified} with status \\emph{completed}. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.outcome]]\nQualification certifies that the run was complete and valid (full declared transcript, trusted-refit scoring, non-vacuous controls, all reference policies computed); it is not evidence of superiority or of generality. Candidate-reported diagnostics are marked unverified by the evaluator and are not used here. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n\n\\subsection{Edit success differs strongly across evaluation designs}\nThe family-averaged edit success score of the first-order influence search is $0.606$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nPanel (a) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} shows the per-design scores: crowd votes $1.000$, sports $0.833$, expert human $0.374$ and LLM judge $0.216$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_crowd]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_sports]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_expert_human]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_llm_judge]]\nBecause a level scores at most one and exactly one only when rank 1 is displaced (up to an exact tie), a design score of $1.000$ means that rank 1 was displaced at every one of the five budgets, including the smallest (0.1\\% of rows), in both crowd cases. The sports score of $0.833<1$ implies that at least one smaller budget in at least one sports case did not displace rank 1. For the LLM judge, rank 1 was not displaced at the 2\\% budget in either mode (Table~\\ref{tab:disp}), so the score of $0.216$ reflects partial closure of the gap.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/design_scores.pdf}\n\\caption{Edit success score on the sealed final data (higher means rank 1 was displaced more reliably or a larger fraction of the gap was closed). (a) The first-order influence search for each evaluation design. (b) Family-averaged scores (equal weights over the four designs) for the evaluator's reference policies and the studied search; blue marks the studied search. Each bar is a single deterministic evaluation value; no uncertainty interval exists.}\n\\label{fig:scores}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Top-1 displacement at the largest budget}\nTable~\\ref{tab:disp} reports the displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by design and edit mode. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_crowd_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_expert_human_flip]]\nRank 1 is displaced in every crowd and sports case in both modes. For expert human data only reversal succeeds, and for the LLM judge neither mode does. Because each crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge cell contains a single case and the proposer is deterministic, every entry is $0$ or $1$; the sports entries average three seasons and also happen to be $0$ or $1$.\nThe ordering by edit success score (crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge) is thus mirrored by the number of design--mode cells in which the heuristic reaches displacement at 2\\% (2, 2, 1, 0 of 2).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: per-design edit success score and top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by edit mode. Entries in the displacement columns are the means over cases and seeds in the cell (one case for crowd, expert human and LLM judge; three seasons for sports).}\n\\label{tab:disp}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nDesign & Cases & Edit success & Displaced at 2\\%, drop & Displaced at 2\\%, flip \\\\\n\\midrule\nCrowd votes  & 2 & 1.000 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_crowd_flip]]\nSports       & 6 & 0.833 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_flip]]\nExpert human & 2 & 0.374 & 0.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_expert_human_drop]]\nLLM judge    & 2 & 0.216 & 0.0 & 0.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_llm_judge_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_llm_judge_flip]]\n\\midrule\nFamily average & 12 & 0.606 & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{not defined} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reference policies set the scale}\nPanel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small fraction of the gap. This suggests that the displacement obtained in crowd and sports data owes more to targeted row selection than to perturbation in general, although per-design values for the random policy are not available to check it. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_constant]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_random]]\nThe win-rate heuristic, which needs no model refit, scores $0.574$ against $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_winrate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_influence]]\nThis is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search (both score $0.606$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nThe task protocol declares a separate claim of outperforming the reference policies, which requires a positive margin and a paired-bootstrap interval excluding zero; that claim is not met here. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#protocol.promotion_rule]]\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks and run-to-run behaviour}\nThe valid proposal rate is $1.0$: every answer to every budget level passed the format checks. The permuted-input control gap is $0.0$, so on the row-shuffled, relabelled copies the proposer obtained the same scores as on the original order, and the measured scores do not depend on row order or competitor labels. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.valid_proposal_rate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.perm_control_abs_gap]]\nThe three evaluation seeds produce identical family-averaged scores ($0.6057$ each), as expected for a deterministic proposer on fixed data. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nThis agreement should not be read as low variability of the leaderboard under resampling: no bootstrap, subsampling or noise model was applied to the data, so we report no confidence interval.\n\n\\section{What the Evidence Does and Does Not Show}\n\\label{sec:analysis}\n\\subsection{A measured ordering, not a general sensitivity law}\nThe measurement supports the statement that, with this heuristic, within these budgets and on these twelve cases, rank-1 vulnerability decreases in the order crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge. The ordering is an observation for one attack and one target. Failure to displace rank 1 (LLM judge in both modes, expert-human data in drop mode) shows only that this one-shot first-order heuristic did not succeed within 2\\% of the rows. It is not a certificate that no successful edit set exists, and a stronger search could close the gap in cells where this one did not. Conversely, success in crowd and sports data shows that rank 1 can be displaced with at most the budget used, not that it is the smallest such edit set.\n\n\\subsection{Candidate explanations, all untested}\nThe artifacts contain no experiment that connects the ordering to a mechanism. We list candidate explanations to guide follow-up work and label each as a hypothesis.\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{Margin relative to data mass.} The relative size of the rank-1 gap compared with the total information in the data determines how many rows must move; designs with a thin top margin would be easier to dislodge. Gaps and row counts per design are not available to us, so this is unchecked.\n\\item \\emph{Concentration of influence.} If the coefficients $s_i$ concentrate on few pairs (for example, matches between the top two competitors), few edits can matter; how concentrated they are depends on the comparison graph of each design, which we did not inspect.\n\\item \\emph{Number of competitors and graph density.} MT-Bench data involve six models, whereas the crowd and sports data involve more competitors. Whether the number of competitors helps or hinders displacement is not established by four designs.\n\\item \\emph{Tie handling.} Ties count as half a win and cannot be reversed in flip mode. Designs with many ties have fewer rows available for reversal; we did not tabulate tie fractions, so we cannot say whether this matters here.\n\\item \\emph{Linearisation error.} A 2\\% edit of a small dataset is a larger perturbation of $A$ and $p$ than a 2\\% edit of a large one, so the additive first-order approximation may degrade differently across designs.\n\\end{itemize}\nDesigns are confounded: they differ at the same time in dataset size, number of competitors, rank-1 margin, tie rate and judge type, and each design has few cases. No single factor can be isolated from this experiment, and the sequentially tuned, single-configuration setup provides no ablations.\n\n\\subsection{Expert human versus GPT-4 judge on the same questions}\nExpert human and GPT-4 judgments are on the same MT-Bench questions and models, which makes this the cleanest contrast in the study. For expert human data, reversal displaces rank 1 at 2\\% while dropping does not; for GPT-4 judgments neither works, and the edit success score is lower ($0.216$ versus $0.374$). The cause is untested. Differences in the number of judgments, the fraction of ties (including the second tie label in the GPT-4 data), and the size of the rank-1 gap are all plausible, and each design has one case per mode, so this is a difference between two single measurements and not a statistical result about human versus LLM judges.\n\n\\subsection{Drop versus reversal}\nIn the one design where the two modes differ (expert human), reversal succeeds and dropping does not. This is consistent with property (iii) of Section~\\ref{sec:influence}: for the same rows, reversal has a larger first-order effect than removal by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|$. It remains a single-cell observation: in crowd and sports data both modes displace rank 1 and in LLM-judge data neither does, so the mode matters in only one of the four designs.\n\n\\subsection{What a stronger test of sensitivity would need}\nOur results leave the original question (sensitivity to small changes from noise or from intent) only partly answered. Answering it would require, at least: edit-size curves for minimal displacing sets from a search with a verified lower-bound or certificate, not only a one-shot heuristic; noise calibration against resampled data (for example, comparing edit sizes with the sampling variability of the gap); several cases per design with confidence intervals; and ablations over subsample size and tie handling. None of these was performed here, and we state them as open rather than as completed work.\n\n\\section{Limitations}\n\\label{sec:limits}\n\\paragraph{One attack, one target.} Only a one-shot first-order search with a single rival direction at the initial fit is evaluated, and only the displacement of rank 1 is targeted. Other targets (top-$m$ sets, pairwise order of two chosen competitors) and iterative or batched searches are not covered.\n\\paragraph{Fixed leaderboard specification.} The evaluator's BT specification (ridge $10^{-3}$, tie as half a win) is part of the measurement. We do not know how conclusions change for other regularisation strengths, tie models or rating systems.\n\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.\n\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty and no noise-versus-intent attribution.} The identical seeds reflect determinism, and no resampling was performed; we therefore cannot say whether the observed vulnerability exceeds ordinary sampling variation of the leaderboard, nor whether it corresponds to plausible noise or to deliberate manipulation. We also do not model what an adversary could actually do on a real platform.\n\\paragraph{Missing diagnostics and aggregate-only baselines.} Per-case gaps, row counts, budget-level scores and per-design values for the reference policies were not available to the analysis, and the evaluator marks candidate diagnostics as unverified. The comparison with the win-rate heuristic is therefore a single aggregate difference.\n\\paragraph{No improved search.} Three alternative search designs were not scored (Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}), so no statement about improved or minimal edit sets can be drawn.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nWe measured, with exact-refit verification, how a one-shot first-order influence search fares at displacing the rank-1 competitor of a ridge Bradley--Terry leaderboard using 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows, on crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and sports data. The family-averaged edit success is 0.606, and it varies strongly by design: 1.000 for crowd votes, 0.833 for sports, 0.374 for expert human data and 0.216 for the LLM judge. At 2\\% the heuristic displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, never for the LLM judge, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes. A win-rate heuristic reaches 0.574, a difference too small to rank the two policies without uncertainty estimates. Qualification of the evaluation attests to its validity, not to superiority or generality; the margin over the best reference policy is zero because the studied search is the evaluator's influence baseline.\nThe result is a bounded measurement for one probe, one target and four datasets. It shows that rank-1 vulnerability under this attack is highly heterogeneous across evaluation designs, which argues against reporting a single sensitivity number for leaderboards. It does not show why, does not separate noise from intent, and does not show minimal edit sizes. Resampling-based calibration, several cases per design and stronger searches are the natural next steps.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluator Specification and Metric Definitions}\n\\label{app:eval}\nThe evaluation interface is a request--response protocol with a reset operation (clearing all proposer state and seeding it) and a propose operation. Each propose request carries the mode, the budget $k$, the number of competitors, the number of rows and base64-encoded integer arrays of competitor indices and outcomes, with rows in the order of the original data. Each call has a hard wall-clock limit of 30 seconds, and the proposer runs on CPU with two threads. Responses with extra keys, non-integer or duplicate or out-of-range indices, more than $k$ indices, or tie rows in flip mode are invalid and score zero for that level. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\nThe sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#CASE_SPECS]]\nDesign scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case, and the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\n\n\\subsection{Complete Final Metric Table}\n\\label{app:metrics}\nTable~\\ref{tab:allmetrics} lists all retained sealed-final metrics.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{All retained metrics of the sealed final evaluation (three evaluation seeds, identical across seeds). Displacement rates are at the 2\\% budget.}\n\\label{tab:allmetrics}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nFamily-averaged edit success (influence search) & 0.6057 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.combined_score]]\nEdit success: crowd / sports & 1.0000 / 0.8328 \\\\\nEdit success: expert human / LLM judge & 0.3742 / 0.2157 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\nDisplacement, drop: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 0 / 0 \\\\\nDisplacement, flip: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 1 / 0 \\\\\nReference policy, constant (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\nReference policy, random (family-averaged) & 0.0709 \\\\\nReference policy, win-rate heuristic (family-averaged) & 0.5739 \\\\\nReference policy, influence baseline (family-averaged) & 0.6057 \\\\\nMargin versus best reference policy & 0.0000 \\\\\nPermuted-input control, mean absolute gap & 0.0000 \\\\\nValid proposal rate & 1.0000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Seeded Evaluation Records}\n\\label{app:seeds}\nThree seed-level records were produced (seeds 201, 202, 203), each with the identical family-averaged score $0.6057$ and recorded durations of $23.1$, $11.8$ and $11.6$ seconds. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\nThe identical scores are expected: the proposer is deterministic, a seed never selects data or workload, and the anonymisation of competitor indices is fixed for each case by the evaluator. Durations vary by a factor of two between records and we do not attribute the difference to any cause. The evaluator labels candidate-produced diagnostics as unverified; none of our statements relies on them, and all quantities are computed by the trusted refit.\n\n\\subsection{Algorithm: First-Order Influence Edit Search}\n\\label{app:algo}\nGiven the arrays, mode and budget $k$:\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item Aggregate rows into a $K\\times K$ weighted win-count matrix (a tie adds $\\tfrac12$ to each direction) and solve Eq.~\\eqref{eq:bt} by Newton iterations (at most 100) until the largest gradient entry falls below $10^{-10}$.\n\\item Take $\\mathrm{top}$ and $\\mathrm{rv}$ as the two largest scores, and solve $A v=e_{\\mathrm{top}}-e_{\\mathrm{rv}}$.\n\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline share this logic. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\nIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n\n\\subsection{Selection Context and Unscored Search Variants}\n\\label{app:selection}\nThe configuration studied here was chosen on a separate search data collection, on which it obtained a search-scope score of $0.914$ with a score delta of $0.0$ relative to the baseline (which is the selected configuration); the study outcome is recorded as completed without a qualifying winner and the selection kind is the baseline. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.investigation_outcome]]\nThree search variants were proposed as potential improvements: a calibrated minimal-perturbation sensitivity analysis across designs, a pair-class aggregated multi-rival greedy search with exact-refit selection, and rival-specific chunked re-linearised edit plans with an exact-refit prefix line search. All three produced no score ($3$ total, $0$ scored, $3$ unscored). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\nUnscored variants are missing performance evidence, not measured losses, and no comparison involving them appears anywhere in this paper. The search-scope score $0.914$ and the final score $0.606$ come from different data collections and scopes, with different designs (the search collection has no expert-human or LLM-judge data); they must not be read as a decline or a generalisation gap.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n\\label{app:llm}\nLarge language models were used throughout this work. An LLM-based research agent designed the three alternative search variants described above and wrote their code, none of which was scored; the studied search is the task's starter code. An LLM agent retrieved references through a literature-search API, planned the paper, wrote this manuscript and its claim-to-evidence mapping from the recorded evaluation artifacts, and produced the figures. The evaluation protocol, data, evaluator and scoring were fixed by the task specification before the run, and every number in the paper is read from the evaluator's recorded outputs. The starter references named in the task were taken from the task description.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":319,"startLine":1,"totalLines":319}}}
