{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNENanDgVZdHz2dQ3VT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_017hgwtsb3R58R9DexhSyeVx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\ns=open('paper.tex').read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:50]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(r\"Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, multivariate responses \\citep{Davidson1969MultivariatePC}, two-dimensional score structure \\citep{Causeur2005A2E}, sparse grouped comparisons \\citep{Yan2011GROUPEDSP}, covariates\",\n    r\"Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates\")\nrep(r\"Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. The model\",\n    r\"Bayesian priors \\citep{Leonard1977AnAB}. Sports results are commonly rated with Elo-style systems that are closely related to BT-type likelihoods; recent generalisations model margins of victory \\citep{Szczecinski2020GElo} and use the likelihood score as the update direction \\citep{Holy2026ScoreDriven}. We fit a static BT model to NBA game outcomes only as a sports instance of pairwise-comparison data, and do not evaluate any Elo-style updating rule. The model\")\nrep(r\"so we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\",\"\")if False else None\nrep(r\"As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\",\n    r\"The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, for which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} give finite-sample error bounds under explicit assumptions and show conditions under which it estimates leave-$k$-out refits consistently for fixed $k$; accuracy is therefore a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, not guaranteed. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\")\nrep(r\"\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\",\n    r\"\"\"\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\nChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research, in which an attacker alters a fraction of the training data \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} study label-flip attacks on log-linear direct preference optimisation, observing that flipping one preference label shifts the gradient independently of the parameters, which turns the choice of flips into a sparse-approximation problem. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\"\"\")\nrep(r\"so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row.\",\n    r\"so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row; the finite-sample error bounds for the underlying linear approximation depend on problem-dependent quantities \\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate them for these data.\")\nrep(r\"Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first &\",\n    r\"Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first &\")\nopen('paper.tex','w').write(s)\nE\npython3 - <<'E'\nimport re\ns=open('/workspace/paper/scripts/build_claims.py').read()\ns=s.replace('\"Davidson1969MultivariatePC\",','').replace('\"Causeur2005A2E\",\"Yan2011GROUPEDSP\",','')\ns=s.replace('''add(\"Related work on influence functions''','''add(\"Related work on infinitesimal-jackknife accuracy, poisoning and label-flip attacks on preference data, and Elo-style sports rating (qualitative positioning).\",[\"ref.openalex.W2896388162\",\"ref.openalex.W7160306608\",\"ref.openalex.W4287201019\",\"ref.openalex.W3211957373\",\"ref.openalex.W7153982762\"],[\"Giordano2018Swiss\",\"Yang2026Preference\",\"Seetharaman2021Influence\",\"Szczecinski2020GElo\",\"Holy2026ScoreDriven\"])\nadd(\"Related work on influence functions''')\nopen('/workspace/paper/scripts/build_claims.py','w').write(s)\nE\ncd /workspace/paper; grep -n 'Davidson1969\\|Causeur\\|Yan2011' scripts/build_claims.py | cut -c1-40; python3 scripts/build_claims.py; python3 -c \"\nimport json;c=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'))['claims'];print(len(c))\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1281,"cache_read_input_tokens":86090,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1281,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de3ecb5e-ff4d-4189-a40b-6bc7f84aa456","uuid":"5d669808-f590-4a80-8307-e437201e3f98","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:45:08.355Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNEMVZ4SJF8SKbrRJZk"}
